::ALG1_U04_PT_Q01::
Question ID: ALG1_U04_PT_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.1
Question:
Which relation is a function?
{
=A. (1,4), (2,6), (3,8)#Correct. Each input appears once and has exactly one output.
~B. (1,4), (1,6), (2,8)#Input 1 has two different outputs, so this is not a function.
~C. (2,5), (2,5), (2,7)#Input 2 is paired with more than one output value.
~D. (3,1), (4,2), (3,5)#Input 3 has two different outputs.
}

::ALG1_U04_PT_Q02::
Question ID: ALG1_U04_PT_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.1
Question:
What does f(4)=11 mean?
{
~A. The input is 11 and the output is 4.#This reverses the input and output in the notation.
~B. The function always equals 11.#The statement gives one output, not every output.
=C. When the input is 4, the output is 11.#Correct. Function notation gives the output for a named input.
~D. The function has a zero at 4.#A zero would mean f(4)=0, not 11.
}

::ALG1_U04_PT_Q03::
Question ID: ALG1_U04_PT_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.2
Question:
If g(x)=3x+2, what is g(5)?
{
~A. 10#This multiplies 2 by 5 instead of substituting into 3x+2.
=B. 17#Correct. g(5)=3(5)+2=15+2=17.
~C. 15#This stops after 3(5) and leaves out the +2.
~D. 25#This multiplies 5 by both numbers instead of following the rule.
}

::ALG1_U04_PT_Q04::
Question ID: ALG1_U04_PT_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.2
Question:
The table shows x: 0, 1, 2, 3 and h(x): 7, 10, 13, 16. What is h(2)?
{
~A. 2#This is the input, not the output.
~B. 10#This output is paired with x = 1.
~C. 16#This output is paired with x = 3.
=D. 13#Correct. The table pairs input 2 with output 13.
}

::ALG1_U04_PT_Q05::
Question ID: ALG1_U04_PT_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.1
Question:
What is the domain of (2,9), (4,11), (6,13)?
{
~A. (9, 11, 13)#These are output values, so they form the range.
=B. (2, 4, 6)#Correct. The domain is the set of input values.
~C. (2, 9, 4, 11, 6, 13)#This combines inputs and outputs instead of listing only inputs.
~D. (2, 6, 9, 13)#This mixes input and output values and leaves some out.
}

::ALG1_U04_PT_Q06::
Question ID: ALG1_U04_PT_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.3
Question:
A graph crosses the x-axis at x=8. What feature is x=8?
{
=A. zero#Correct. A zero is an input where the output equals 0.
~B. y-intercept#The y-intercept happens when x = 0.
~C. maximum#An x-axis crossing does not necessarily show the highest output.
~D. starting value#The starting value is the output when the input is 0.
}

::ALG1_U04_PT_Q07::
Question ID: ALG1_U04_PT_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.8
Question:
What is the minimum value of y=|x-6|?
{
~A. 6#The graph is centered at x = 6, but the output there is 0.
~B. -6#Absolute value outputs are not negative.
=C. 0#Correct. Absolute value represents distance, and the smallest possible distance is 0.
~D. 12#This can be an output for some inputs, but it is not the minimum.
}

::ALG1_U04_PT_Q08::
Question ID: ALG1_U04_PT_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.4
Question:
Which table matches y=2x+5?
{
~A. x: 0, 1, 2; y: 2, 7, 12#This starts at 2 and increases by 5, reversing the slope and intercept.
~B. x: 0, 1, 2; y: 5, 6, 7#This starts at 5 but increases by 1, not 2.
~C. x: 0, 1, 2; y: 0, 2, 4#This matches 2x, not 2x+5.
=D. x: 0, 1, 2; y: 5, 7, 9#Correct. Substitution gives 5, 7, and 9.
}

::ALG1_U04_PT_Q09::
Question ID: ALG1_U04_PT_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.LR.4
Question:
Function A is A(x)=4x+3. Function B is B(x)=2x+9. Which function has the greater rate of change?
{
=A. Function A#Correct. Function A has slope 4, and Function B has slope 2.
~B. Function B#Function B has the greater y-intercept, but the smaller slope.
~C. They have the same rate of change.#The slopes are 4 and 2, so the rates are different.
~D. Cannot be determined.#Both equations show the rates of change.
}

::ALG1_U04_PT_Q10::
Question ID: ALG1_U04_PT_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.4
Question:
A context says there is a $10 starting fee plus $3 per item. Which equation matches the context?
{
~A. C(x)=10x+3#This reverses the starting fee and per-item rate.
=B. C(x)=3x+10#Correct. The starting fee is 10 and each item adds 3.
~C. C(x)=13x#This treats the fee and item cost as one rate.
~D. C(x)=3x-10#The starting fee is added, not subtracted.
}

